rishi sunak is the former investment  bank analyst now running the uk's  treasury and widely regarded as a likely  successor to the current prime minister  boris johnson when the uk went into  covet lockdown in 2020 sunac's  government-backed furlough scheme  subsidize the salaries of more than 11  million workers at a cost of 95 billion  dollars  now he says it's time to start balancing  the books  i have to be blunt with you our recovery  comes with a cost  our national debt is almost a hundred  percent of gdp  so we need to fix our public finances so  this week low income families will stop  receiving a temporary twenty pounds or  27 us dollars a week uplift to a social  benefit called universal credit and  taxes are going up too it's going to be  a very tough winter for some  my great worry is that we're going to  have lots more people pushed into the  arms of loan sharks forced to use food  banks and actually that's unnecessary  you know the government could just  continue with the 20 pound a week uplift  into the winter into the spring and  summer next year and that would just  alleviate a huge amount of financial  pressure on low income families across  the country including here in greater  manchester on monday the army began  helping to deliver fuel to petrol  filling stations a very visible sign of  the uk's chronic shortage of truck  drivers the situation which has caused  petrol pumps to run dry and also seen  empty shelves in supermarkets  many economists are warning that the  united kingdom faces a cost of living  crisis in the coming months as energy  prices go up and the government claws  back some of the benefits it paid out  during the pandemic what's not clear is  whether the chancellor's proposed ways  of mitigating that damage are enough in  deprived areas such as salford  proposals include a scheme called  kickstart to help young low-income  workers and incentives for companies to  take on apprentices  in his salford workshop mike cleary says  he's just about getting by  we're ticking over that's the main thing  uh we could be we could do a lot better  what we are doing  but again without proper training  without  the bully being pumped into  apprenticeship scheme  then it's welcome to a stand still  next month rishi sunak delivers a formal  budget which will detail the  government's financial plans but the  indications are that for many in the uk  things are going to get worse before  they might get better  paul brennan al jazeera manchester
 

